<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">South Yemen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1967">1967</a>–<a href="/years/1990">1990</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as People's Democratic Republic of Yemen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, from 1970 the People's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-yemen">Democratic Republic of Yemen</a>, was the state formed in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> withdrew from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-arabia">South Arabian</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorates</a> in November 1967. Ruled by the Marxist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemeni-socialist-party">Yemeni Socialist Party</a>, it was the only communist state in the Arab world and was closely allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. Weakened by the civil war of 1986, it united with North Yemen in 1990.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/south-yemen.jpg" alt="South Yemen" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: National Front - الجبهة القومية · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_South_Yemen.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
